    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Bukhara grew from three small fortified settlements of the Sogdian oasis into a walled city by 500 BCE, and passed from Persian rule to Alexander, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, the Greco-Bactrians, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> and the Turkic Kaganate, and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, who took it after long resistance in the 8th century.</li><li>As a capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> it became an intellectual centre of the Islamic world, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a> built the Kalan Minaret, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> took the city in 1220 after a siege of fifteen days.</li><li>The Uzbek Khanate and the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bukhara ruled it until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> attacked in 1920, and in 1924 the city became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> Soviet Socialist Republic.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narshakhi">Narshakhi</a>'s History of Bukhara, completed in 943 to 944, records a fair at the bazaar of Makh at which idols were bought and sold, a practice that continued even after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Islamic conquest</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

