    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 500 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Three small fortified settlements have grown together and been enclosed by a wall, and Bukhara enters history as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the Persian Empire</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">712</span><span class="rf-text">Qutaiba ibn Muslim builds a grand mosque inside the citadel, on the site of a former temple</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">9th century</span><span class="rf-text">Bukhara becomes the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanid Empire</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">999</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a> topple the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1220</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> besieges Bukhara for fifteen days, and most of the city burns</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1785</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-murad">Shah Murad</a> formalises the dynastic rule of the Manghit family and the khanate becomes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bukhara</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1920</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze">Mikhail Frunze</a> attack the city, and the emir flees</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1924</span><span class="rf-text">The Bukharan People's Soviet Republic is integrated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> Soviet Socialist Republic</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/siyavash"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siyavash.jpg" alt="Siyavash" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/siyavash">Siyavash</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siyavush_(The_Shahnama_of_Shah_Tahmasp).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bukhara stands on the Zeravshan River in south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, at an elevation of 229 metres. Its climate is cool and arid, with a mean annual precipitation of 135 millimetres, so from ancient times the oasis depended on irrigation, on canals and on uncovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> called hauzes. The oasis had been inhabited long before the city existed. The archaeologist E. E. Kuzmina links the Zaman-Baba culture of the third millennium BCE to the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-aryans">Indo-Aryans</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and from 3000 BCE a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> culture, the Sapalli, thrived at sites such as Varakhsha, Vardan, Paykend and Ramitan.</p><p>Around 1500 BCE climatic drying, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">iron technology</a> and the arrival of Indo-Aryan nomads triggered a population shift to the oasis. By 1000 BCE the Sapalli and the Indo-Aryan people had merged into one culture, and around 700 BCE this culture, called Sogdian, flourished in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> along the Zeravshan Valley. Three small fortified settlements had been built by then, and by 500 BCE they had grown together and were enclosed by a wall. The city is officially said to have been founded in 500 BCE in the area now called the Ark.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> gives another account. In the epic the city was founded by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyavash">Siavash</a>, who built the Ark and the surrounding city in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdom granted to him by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrasiab">Afrasiab</a>, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eucratides-i.jpg" alt="Eucratides I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eucratides_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Eucratides I / BnF, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bukhara entered history in 500 BCE as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the Persian Empire. It was a cult centre for the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a>, and about once in a lunar cycle the inhabitants of the Zeravshan Fan exchanged their old idols of the goddess for new ones at a trade festival held in front of the Mokh Temple. The festival made Bukhara a centre of commerce. A gold 20-stater of the Greco-Bactrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucratides-i">Eucratides I</a>, the largest gold coin ever minted in antiquity at 169.2 grams, was found in the city.</p><p>Trade quickened on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> pushed back the northern tribes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> trade encouraged the rapid growth of the city, which ended around 350 CE. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> the city entered a steep decline, but the 5th century saw unprecedented growth in urban and rural settlements throughout the oasis, which was surrounded by a wall more than 400 km long.</p><p>The city reached its greatest intellectual standing under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, when it was renowned for its numerous libraries and became a rival to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudaki">Rudaki</a>, called the father of Persian poetry, was born and raised there. The Samanid slave trade was the main income of the state. In 1070 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-shenzong-of-song">Shenzong</a> invited 5,300 Muslim men from Bukhara to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, and in 1080 a further 10,000 Muslims came.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bukhara was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the Persian Empire from 500 BCE, and then passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, the Greco-Bactrians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>. After the Kushan Empire it fell to Hua tribes from the steppe. Between 563 and 567 the Turks of the Turkic Kaganate defeated the Hephtalites in a battle near the city, and Bukhara became dependent on the Kaganate. Arab armies arrived in 650 and found it ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhar-khudah">Bukhar Khudahs</a>, the Lords of Bukhara. Qutaiba ibn Muslim finally seized the city, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> did not truly conquer it until after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> in 751.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> made it their capital in the 9th century and held it until 999, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Karakhanids</a> toppled them. The city was included in the Karakhanid state in 1005. It then belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> Shahs until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> took it in 1220, and afterwards to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timurid-empire">Timurid Empire</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a> took its name when the capital of the Shaybanid state was moved there, and it reached its greatest extent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-khan-ii">Abdullah Khan II</a>, who ruled from 1577 to 1598. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nadir Shah</a> conquered it in 1740. After his death the khanate was controlled by the descendants of Khudayar Bi through the office of ataliq, and in 1785 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-murad">Shah Murad</a> formalised the family's dynastic rule, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghit dynasty</a>, and the khanate became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bukhara. The emirate was a colonial acquisition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/narshakhi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/narshakhi.jpg" alt="Narshakhi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/narshakhi">Narshakhi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DeBoukhara_Cover.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ernest Leroux, 1892, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bukhara was multi-ethnic and multi-religious. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> arrived in 650 they found a decentralised collection of peoples, and before the Arab invasion the city sheltered followers of two religious movements persecuted within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, Manicheanism and Nestorian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. Many coins with Christian symbols have been found in and around Bukhara, more than anywhere else in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narshakhi">Narshakhi</a> recorded that the people became Muslims but apostatised each time the Muslims withdrew, until Qutaiba established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> there after much difficulty. For a century after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-talas">Battle of Talas</a> Islam took root slowly. The Bukharan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, whose ancestors settled in the city during Roman times, mostly left between 1925 and 2000.</p><p>The city was a centre of learning. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a> New Persian flourished there, and a Samanid edict declared that in this region the language was Persian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">Imam</a> Bukhari, the compiler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadith">hadiths</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avicenna">Avicenna</a> lived there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-khayyam">Omar Khayyam</a> worked in Bukhara for a time at the invitation of the Karakhanid ruler Shams al-Mulk, and Baha-ud-Din Naqshband, a central figure of the Naqshbandi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order, came from one of its neighbourhoods.</p><p>Water came from ponds, the hauzes, which also spread disease and were mostly filled in by the Soviets in the 1920s and 1930s. The Lab-i Hauz survived as the centrepiece of an ensemble of the 16th and 17th centuries. For several centuries Bukhara and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khiva">Khiva</a> were major centres of the slave trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genghis-khan.jpg" alt="Genghis Khan" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YuanEmperorAlbumGenghisPortrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> Shahs, who held Bukhara, had killed the ambassador of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, and in 1220 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> besieged the city for fifteen days. According to Juvaini, Genghis Khan contented himself with looting and slaughter once and did not go to the extreme of a general massacre, because the population readily submitted while the garrison in the citadel resisted. Most of the city burned. He spared most adults, killed 30,000 Qangli Turks on account of their loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Muhammad, and conscripted all remaining able-bodied men.</p><p>The city recovered slowly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, passing through the region around 1333, found that the town had not recovered from being sacked, that its mosques, colleges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> were in ruins, and that no one in it had religious learning or showed concern for acquiring it. By the 19th century it had become a peripheral city of the Persian and Islamic world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stoddart">Charles Stoddart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conolly">Arthur Conolly</a>, held there by the emir during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-great-game">Great Game</a>, were thrown into a vermin pit for months and then beheaded outside the citadel.</p><p>In 1920, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze">Mikhail Frunze</a> attacked the city. On 31 August the emir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-alim-khan">Mohammed Alim Khan</a>, fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a>, and on 2 September, after four days of fighting, the Ark was destroyed and the red flag was raised from the top of the Kalan Minaret. In 1922 most of Bukhara temporarily fell into the hands of the Basmachis during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-pasha">Enver Pasha</a>'s campaign.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bukhara has about 140 architectural monuments, and its historic centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-ibn-ahmad">Ismail Samani</a> mausoleum, built between 892 and 943, combines Zoroastrian and Islamic motifs, and the mausoleum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> was modelled after it. It was said to have been buried in mud from flooding when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> came, so that they spared it. The Karakhanid ruler Arslan Khan ordered the Kalan Minaret, which is 45.6 metres high and was designed by an architect named Bako. The Magok-i-Attari Mosque has a south facade of the 12th century, one of the oldest surviving structures in the city.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> period added the Kalan Mosque, arguably completed in 1514 and able to hold twelve thousand people, the Mir-i Arab Madrasah of 1535 to 1536, and the Lab-i Hauz ensemble of 1568 to 1622. The Bolo Haouz Mosque was built in 1712. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>' Shirbudun Palace was begun about 1870.</p><p>The city entered literature as Albracca in Boiardo's epic Orlando Innamorato of 1483. Bukhara is today the capital of Bukhara Region, and one of the two major centres, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>'s Tajik minority. Its population was recorded as 279,200 in 2019.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Bukhara, on the Zeravshan River in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>, was officially founded in 500 BCE and became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> trade centre and, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samanid-empire">Samanids</a>, an intellectual centre of the Islamic world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> took it in 1220, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Bukhara ended in 1920.</em></p>

